{"language":"FCL-1","records":[{"id":"n1","type":"claim","text":"No text of the selected previous account (h005.fork5.p.account.0) is exposed in this invocation; this account therefore begins rather than revises."},{"id":"c1","type":"claim","text":"The repeated later disagreement about what an agreement meant indicates under-specified agreements (no shared what, when, or definition of done), not necessarily bad faith.","grounds":"The task states that the flatmates sometimes agree on who will do something but later disagree about what the agreement meant."},{"id":"c2","type":"claim","text":"Because work schedules change, any fixed rota or standing assignment will keep decaying; the allocation needs a renegotiation cadence or schedule-triggered rules."},{"id":"c3","type":"claim","text":"The friend avoiding the conversations most plausibly finds the conversations themselves costly (they feel like blame or conflict); the exposed material does not establish the actual reason.","scope":"conjecture"},{"id":"a1","type":"commitment","text":"Propose one short, time-boxed house meeting framed as fixing the system rather than reviewing failures.","action":"convene the meeting with that explicit framing","consequence":"process design is separated from blame incidents"},{"id":"a2","type":"commitment","text":"Record every agreement in writing in a place all three can see, stated in checkable terms: task, deadline, and what done looks like.","action":"set up a shared note, board, or group-chat record","consequence":"later disputes can be checked against the record instead of memory","depends":["c1"]},{"id":"a3","type":"commitment","text":"Replace the fixed rota with a weekly ten-minute re-allocation against actual schedules, or with trigger-based rules such as first-one-home starts dinner.","action":"adopt one renegotiation cadence or trigger set","consequence":"allocation tracks changing schedules without ad-hoc conflict at the scene of a missed chore","depends":["c2"]},{"id":"a4","type":"commitment","text":"Approach the avoidant friend one-on-one, off the topic of any specific chore, ask what would make the talks easier, and offer an asynchronous written channel.","action":"private conversation plus async option","consequence":"re-includes the third person without forcing the format they are avoiding","depends":["c3"]},{"id":"a5","type":"commitment","text":"If failures persist, negotiate explicit minimum standards, allowing trades across chore domains if standards differ.","action":"standards negotiation with possible cross-domain trades","consequence":"addresses a standards mismatch that allocation fixes cannot reach"},{"id":"o1","type":"objection","text":"Formalizing chores in writing can feel bureaucratic and can relocate the dispute from the chore to the wording of the record.","target":["a2"],"bearing":"If this holds, the record must be kept minimal and treated as revisable rather than as law."},{"id":"p1","type":"problem","text":"What is the avoidant friend actually avoiding: conflict, feeling out-voted, a rigged process, or something unrelated to chores? Not determinable from the exposed material."},{"id":"p2","type":"problem","text":"Do the three flatmates share minimum cleanliness standards, or is part of the dispute a genuine standards mismatch?"},{"id":"u1","type":"use","text":"Use claims c1 to c3 and commitments a1 to a5 as the agenda for the proposed house meeting, and carry p1 and p2 as the questions to answer there.","target":["c1","c2","c3","a1","a2","a3","a4","a5","p1","p2"]}],"uptake":["c1","c2","c3","a1","a2","a3","a4","a5","o1","p1","p2","u1"]}